# Who reviews what.
#
# The repository is split by layer and so are the branches: FloraScan-Web-App/
# is the frontend's, Server/ is the backend's, and the schema and scripts/
# belong to whoever owns the data model. Those folders are independent -
# nothing in one imports anything from another - so work in them does not
# need a gatekeeper.
#
# The files below are the exception. They are shared by every layer, and a
# change to one of them can break a branch whose owner never touched it, so
# they get a review from the person doing the connectivity checks.
#
# Note: on GitHub Free, CODEOWNERS only requests reviews automatically for
# PUBLIC repositories. On a private repository it needs Pro, Team or
# Enterprise. Either way this file still records who owns what, which is most
# of the point.

# Build and dependency configuration. Two branches each adding a package
# produce a package-lock.json conflict that is miserable to resolve by hand.
/package.json           @NyChieng
/package-lock.json      @NyChieng
/vite.config.js         @NyChieng

# How the project is set up and run, which everybody follows.
/README.md              @NyChieng
/.env.example           @NyChieng
/.gitignore             @NyChieng

# Both suites test the whole stack, so they belong to nobody in particular.
/tests/                 @NyChieng

# The layer folders. Uncomment and add handles as people take ownership, so
# GitHub requests the right reviewer instead of nobody.
# /FloraScan-Web-App/           @handle
# /Server/                      @handle
# /scripts/                     @handle
# /plant_registry_schema.sql    @handle
